---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import Setting from "../components/Setting.tsx";
import { repo } from "../lib/site";
---

<Prose title="How this works" active="method" description="Every charity is a model file, imported from an evaluator or built by us from public evidence, ranked in years of healthy life per $1,000 under values you set.">
  <p class="lede">
    Every charity is ranked by a model: a public file of numbers and formulas that ends in <strong>years of healthy life per $1,000 donated</strong>. Most models come from charity evaluators; the rest we build. Every model then counts the same things the same way, at values you set.
  </p>

  <ol class="flow" aria-label="From an evaluator's analysis to the rankings">
    <li><span class="flow-n">1</span><strong>An evaluator's analysis</strong><span>imported so it reproduces their numbers, or built by us</span></li>
    <li><span class="flow-n">2</span><strong>One way to count</strong><span>shared figures replace each evaluator's own conversions</span></li>
    <li><span class="flow-n">3</span><strong>Years of healthy life</strong><span>every unit converted, mostly at rates you set</span></li>
    <li><span class="flow-n">4</span><strong>Adjustments</strong><span>old, unmeasured or past-average estimates count less</span></li>
    <li><span class="flow-n">5</span><strong>The rankings</strong><span>per $1,000 given today</span></li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="sources">Where models come from</h2>
  <p>
    Most are <a href="#imported-models">imported</a> from <a href="/evaluators/givewell">GiveWell</a>, <a href="/evaluators/ace">Animal Charity Evaluators</a>, the <a href="/evaluators/hli">Happier Lives Institute</a>, <a href="/evaluators/founders-pledge">Founders Pledge</a>, <a href="/evaluators/ambitious-impact">Ambitious Impact</a>, <a href="/evaluators/sogive">SoGive</a> and <a href="/evaluators">others</a>. Inputs are marked <strong>observed</strong>, <strong>estimate</strong> or <strong>judgment</strong>, and most cite their source; each model's history page shows every change.
  </p>
  <p id="views">
    We keep each evaluator's analysis as published, so it still reproduces, but rank our version of it: the site's shared figures and your values in place of the evaluator's own. A row where we use a different number is marked "changed for consistency"; open it to see the evaluator's. The page of each imported model starts from the evaluator's own final figure and shows, step by step, how it becomes ours.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthy-years">One unit: years of healthy life</h2>
  <p>
    A year of healthy life is one person living one more year in full health (a DALY averted). Evaluators count in other units, so each is converted:
  </p>
  <div class="units">
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">Doubled income for a year</span><span class="unit-by">GiveWell</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A point of life satisfaction for a year</span><span class="unit-by">Happier Lives Institute</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A day of severe pain, in chickens</span><span class="unit-by">Animal evaluators</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A tonne of CO₂ kept out of the air</span><span class="unit-by">Climate evaluators</span></div>
    <div class="unit-to">→ years of healthy life</div>
  </div>
  <Setting client:load ids={["uov_per_daly", "daly_per_child_death"]} />
  <p class="small muted">
    Deaths at other ages count in GiveWell's proportions by age: a school-age child's a little more, an adult's over 50 much less. At the defaults, cash transfers from GiveDirectly come to about 4 healthy years per $1,000, the Against Malaria Foundation to about 23.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wellbeing">Life satisfaction</h3>
  <p>
    The default makes a year of depression one step lighter count the same through life satisfaction as through the disability weights health researchers use, so both routes agree.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["daly_per_wellby"]} />

  <h3 id="animals">Animals</h3>
  <p>
    Days of pain are counted in chickens, other species against a chicken by Rethink Priorities' estimates of how much each can suffer: a pig, cow, dog or cat as 1.6 chickens, a fish 0.2, a shrimp 0.1. Then two values: how many chickens' suffering equals a person's, and what a day of severe pain costs in healthy time.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["chicken_vs_person", "daly_per_sad"]} />
  <p class="small muted">
    The default of 10,000 chickens is what people's own spending on chicken welfare implies; researchers who rate how much a chicken can suffer put it at 3. Where animal evaluators use different numbers for the same change (a hen out of a cage, a meal replaced), every model uses one shared figure.
  </p>

  <h3 id="climate">Climate</h3>
  <p>
    A tonne of CO₂ is valued by the deaths it causes: about 1.9 per 10,000 tonnes (Rethink Priorities), three quarters from the air pollution of burning fuel and a quarter from heat. Pollution deaths come at once, but only tonnes from burning less fuel avert them; heat deaths come about 60 years later and are discounted. These are fixed figures, not sliders. Damage that isn't death, such as lost harvests, counts as zero, so climate figures are probably low.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-way">Adjustments</h2>
  <p>
    The same rules apply to ours and the evaluators' models alike. The evaluator's own final figure, where each model page starts, has none of them.
  </p>

  <h3 id="estimate-age">Old estimates count less</h3>
  <p>
    Evaluators re-check what they recommend every year or two; an estimate nobody renewed is less likely to hold. At 5% a year a 2-year-old estimate counts 91%, a 10-year-old one 61%. Age runs to the date the data was last refreshed, so figures don't drift by themselves.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["estimate_age_discount"]} />

  <h3 id="missing-data">Results nobody measured</h3>
  <p>
    A result counts in full where the charity's own count of who it reached meets an effect measured in trials or its own controlled evaluation. Anything resting on judgment, projections or research on other delivery counts less. GiveWell's top charities are measured; idea-stage and most campaign estimates are not.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["missing_data_discount"]} />

  <h3>Past averages and reforms</h3>
  <p>
    Only for the models these apply to. An organisation's past spending over everything it achieved is an average; the next dollar usually does less. A won reform counts only for the years before it would have come anyway, after the wait until it takes effect.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["marginal_vs_past_average", "win_years_counted"]} />

  <h3 id="near-and-far">The future, and lives far away</h3>
  <p>
    Future good is discounted, a wait before it starts included. Future generations count through the same rate: at its floor of 0.1% a year lost for ever counts about 1,000 years, at 4% about 25. By default a life counts the same wherever it is; set where you live to count lives further away less.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["discount_rate", "home_country", "distance_discount"]} />

  <h3>Credit</h3>
  <p>
    By default each model keeps its evaluator's way of crediting a charity. Switch this on and credit is shared by who paid for what, so the shares add up to the whole and nothing is counted twice.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["resource_shares"]} />

  <h2 id="charity-figure">A charity's figure</h2>
  <p>
    A charity with several programmes, or one programme in several countries, gets the funding-weighted average of them. The Against Malaria Foundation at the defaults, for example:
  </p>
  <div class="eq" aria-label="Against Malaria Foundation: 80% of 24 plus 12% of 22 plus 6% of 8 plus 2% of 12 is about 23">
    <span class="eq-term"><b>80%</b> × 24<small>DRC</small></span><span class="eq-op">+</span>
    <span class="eq-term"><b>12%</b> × 22<small>Nigeria</small></span><span class="eq-op">+</span>
    <span class="eq-term"><b>6%</b> × 8<small>Chad</small></span><span class="eq-op">+</span>
    <span class="eq-term"><b>2%</b> × 12<small>South Sudan</small></span><span class="eq-op">=</span>
    <span class="eq-term eq-result"><b>23</b><small>the charity</small></span>
  </div>
  <p>
    A charity with analyses from several evaluators shows each as its own dot; its number is the newest analysis from the first of them in a fixed order (GiveWell first, ours last). Charities that move money to others (fundraisers, effective-giving groups) are worth what that money does where it goes, read live from those charities' models. Ambitious Impact estimates made before a charity existed are shown only if it runs that programme today, marked <a href="#theoretical">purely theoretical</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ranges">Ranges and warning signs</h2>
  <figure class="range-fig">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 440 64" role="img" aria-labelledby="range-title">
      <title id="range-title">A result's dot on its bar: the dot uses every best guess, the bar runs from every input at its low end to every input at its high end</title>
      <rect x="70" y="24" width="300" height="10" rx="5" class="rf-bar" />
      <circle cx="190" cy="29" r="8" class="rf-dot" />
      <text x="70" y="56" class="rf-t">every input low</text>
      <text x="370" y="56" text-anchor="end" class="rf-t">every input high</text>
      <text x="190" y="14" text-anchor="middle" class="rf-t">best guesses</text>
    </svg>
  </figure>
  <p id="theoretical">
    Rankings use the dot. The bar is deliberately wide, not a 90% interval. A warning sign marks a charity whose range spans over 100×, whose uncertainty is mostly judgment, whose analysis is over 18 months old, or whose model is purely theoretical. On each model page, every number shows its share of the uncertainty; "Reveal uncertainties" opens the ones behind most of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="imported-models">Imported and our own models</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Imported:</strong> every public cost-effectiveness model we found and could read, from GiveWell's grant pages and the other evaluators (the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/inventory`}>inventory</a> lists everything and why anything was left out). Each spreadsheet is traced from its bottom line, and every calculated row must match the sheet, or the build fails. A few evaluators published only report figures, not a sheet; those models rebuild the figures and say so.
  </p>
  <p id="our-models">
    <strong>Ours:</strong> where no evaluator has looked, an AI agent builds the model by a fixed, published method, from the charity's accounts and reports and the research on its kind of work, never another charity's result. It covers every programme the money pays for, weighted by spending, with every number traced to a source or stated as a judgment with a range. A separate AI reviewer then checks it against its sources; it is fixed and checked again, for up to three rounds, and published only once no serious finding is left. Built blind for seven charities evaluators have analysed, four landed within 3× of the evaluator (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/eval-skills.md`}>details</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="judgments">Fixed judgments</h2>
  <p>
    A judgment that concerns only a few models is a fixed figure rather than a slider, such as how long until a tonne's heat deaths come, stand-ins for species Rethink Priorities hasn't estimated (cows, dogs, cats), or the share of corporate pledges companies keep. Each is marked "judgment" wherever it is used, with a range the bars use. Evidence-based rates are listed with sources under <a href="/data">shared data</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>
    Not any evaluator's recommendation, and not endorsed by any evaluator or charity listed. Cost-effectiveness is one input to a giving decision; room for more funding and how well a charity is run matter too, and aren't modelled yet.
  </p>
</Prose>

<style>
  :global(.wrap h3) {
    margin: var(--s5) 0 var(--s2);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 19px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .flow {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0 !important;
    margin: var(--s5) 0 !important;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s2);
  }
  .flow li {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: var(--s3);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--paper-2);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    margin: 0 !important;
  }
  .flow li strong {
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .flow li span:last-child {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .flow-n {
    font: 650 13px/1 var(--sans);
    color: var(--blue);
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .flow {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .units {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s2);
    margin: var(--s4) 0;
  }
  .unit {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    padding: var(--s2) var(--s3);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .unit-from {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .unit-by {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .unit-to {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    font: 650 17px/1.3 var(--serif);
    color: var(--cause-global-health);
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .units {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .eq {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s2);
    margin: var(--s4) 0;
  }
  .eq-term {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    padding: var(--s2) var(--s3);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .eq-term small {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .eq-result {
    border-color: var(--cause-global-health);
  }
  .eq-result b {
    color: var(--cause-global-health);
  }
  .eq-op {
    font: 400 20px/1 var(--serif);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .range-fig {
    margin: var(--s4) 0;
    max-width: 440px;
  }
  .range-fig svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .rf-bar {
    fill: var(--rule);
  }
  .rf-dot {
    fill: var(--cause-global-health);
    stroke: var(--paper);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .rf-t {
    font: 12px var(--sans);
    fill: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
